'''torpify''' (tôr′pi-fī), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''torpified'', ppr. ''torpifying''. [< L. ''torpefacere'', make numb, < ''torpere'', be numb, + ''facere'', make (see ''-fy'').] To make torpid; stupefy; numb; blunt.

{{smaller block|They [sermons] are not harmless if they ''torpify'' the understanding.{{right|''Southey'', Doctor, xxvii.}}}}

'''torpitude''' (tôr′pi-tūd), ''n.'' [Irreg., < ''torpi(d)'' + ''-tude.''] Torpor; torpidity; dormancy, as of animals. See ''torpidity'', 2.

{{smaller block|Able to exist in a kind of ''torpitude'' or sleeping state without any food.{{right|''Derham'', Physico-Theol., viii. 5.}}}}

