'''torpor''' (tôr′po̤r), ''n.'' [= F. ''torpeur'' = Sp. Pg. ''torpor'' = It. ''torpore'', < L. ''torpor'', numbness, < ''torpere'', be numb or torpid: see ''torpent'', ''torpid.''] 1. Loss of motion or sensibility; numbness or inactivity of mind or body; torpidity; torpidness; dormancy; apathy; stupor: as, the ''torpor'' of a hibernating animal; the ''torpor'' of intoxication or of grief.

{{smaller block|It was some time before he [Rip Van Winkle] could get into the regular track of gossip, or could be made to comprehend the strange events that had taken place during his ''torpor.''{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 64.}}}}

2. Dullness; sluggishness; apathy; stupidity.
